1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:06,225
Chapter 42: Causes of Obstetrical Hemorrhage.

3
00:00:06,225 --> 00:00:21,575
General Considerations Uterine Atony Uterine Inversion Birth Canal Injuries Uterine Rupture Amnionic Fluid Embolism References In addition to hypertension and infection, obstetrical hemorrhage remains among the infamous triad of maternal death causes.

4
00:00:21,575 --> 00:00:38,062
Of more than 7000 pregnancy-related maternal deaths in the United States from 2006 to 2015, hemorrhage was a direct cause in 11 percent (Creanga, 2015, 2017; Petersen, 2019).

5
00:00:38,062 --> 00:00:47,600
Hemorrhage is also the single most important cause of maternal death worldwide (Goffman, 2016; Oladapo, 2016).

6
00:00:47,600 --> 00:00:52,775
Notably, perhaps a third of severe cases of hemorrhage are likely preventable .

7
00:00:52,775 --> 00:01:00,237
These statistics have prompted several organizations to develop programs to prevent hemorrhage-related maternal morbidity.

8
00:01:00,237 --> 00:01:13,437
In the United States, one example is the Alliance for Innovation on Maternal Health (AIM) (2015), with its intent to standardize recognition, response, and reporting of obstetrical hemorrhage.

9
00:01:13,437 --> 00:01:24,675
The Joint Commission (2019) has also implemented standards under the Provision of Care, Treatment, and Services chapter for obstetrical hemorrhage—the R3 Report.

10
00:01:24,675 --> 00:01:27,937
Our following three chapters align with these.

11
00:01:27,937 --> 00:01:30,300
General Considerations.

12
00:01:30,300 --> 00:01:33,862
# Mechanisms of Normal Hemostasis.

13
00:01:33,862 --> 00:01:43,112
Near term, an incredible amount of blood—at least 600 mL/min—flows through the spiral arteries and into the intervillous space .

14
00:01:43,112 --> 00:01:52,850
Averaging 120 in number, the spiral arteries lack a muscular layer because of their remodeling by trophoblasts and thereby form a low-pressure system.

15
00:01:52,850 --> 00:01:58,000
With placental separation, vessels at the implantation site are avulsed.

16
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:04,600
Hemostasis is achieved first by myometrial contraction, which directly compresses the arteries.

17
00:02:04,600 --> 00:02:09,900
Compression is followed by clotting and eventually by obliteration of vessel lumens.

18
00:02:09,900 --> 00:02:18,700
If, after delivery, the myometrium contracts vigorously, substantial hemorrhage from the placental implantation site is unlikely.

19
00:02:18,700 --> 00:02:29,287
Importantly, an intact coagulation system is not necessary for postpartum hemostasis unless there are lacerations in the uterus, birth canal, or perineum.

20
00:02:29,287 --> 00:02:35,825
However, fatal postpartum hemorrhage can result from uterine atony despite normal coagulation.

21
00:02:35,825 --> 00:02:38,700
# Definition and Incidence.

22
00:02:38,700 --> 00:02:47,750
Historically, postpartum hemorrhage has been defined as blood losses ≥500 mL after the third stage of labor.

23
00:02:47,750 --> 00:02:55,987
This is problematic because almost half of all women delivered vaginally shed that amount of blood or more when losses are carefully measured.

24
00:02:55,987 --> 00:03:03,550
Moreover, approximately 5 percent of women delivering vaginally lose >1000 mL of blood .

25
00:03:03,550 --> 00:03:10,087
Almost a third of women undergoing cesarean delivery have blood loss >1000 mL.

26
00:03:10,087 --> 00:03:24,662
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019a) now defines postpartum hemorrhage as cumulative blood loss >1000 mL or blood loss accompanied by signs and symptoms of hypovolemia.

27
00:03:24,662 --> 00:03:27,250
Figure 42-1.

28
00:03:27,250 --> 00:03:35,137
Measured blood loss with vaginal delivery, repeat cesarean delivery, and repeat cesarean delivery plus hysterectomy.

29
00:03:35,137 --> 00:03:48,450
In a Maternal-Fetal Network Units study of more than 115,000 deliveries, the incidence of hemorrhage with vaginal delivery was 5.3 percent, and it was 10.5 percent for cesarean delivery .

30
00:03:48,450 --> 00:03:51,775
Importantly, hemorrhage is underreported.

31
00:03:51,775 --> 00:04:02,375
From the National Hospital Discharge Summary database, the reported postpartum hemorrhage incidence between 2001 and 2005 was only 2.6 percent .

32
00:04:02,375 --> 00:04:05,062
# Blood Loss Assessment.

33
00:04:05,062 --> 00:04:11,012
At delivery, visual estimation is often used as a qualitative measure of blood loss.

34
00:04:11,012 --> 00:04:23,762
However, this method is more likely to under estimate the actual blood loss when volumes are high and to over estimate it when volumes are low (Al Kadri, 2011; Natrella, 2018).

35
00:04:23,762 --> 00:04:29,425
Thus, estimated blood loss that is considered greater than “average” should alert the provider.

36
00:04:29,425 --> 00:04:34,087
Instead, different methods can be used as quantitative measures.

37
00:04:34,087 --> 00:04:40,325
First, gravimetric measurement weighs blood-soaked items and subtracts pre-use dry weights.

38
00:04:40,325 --> 00:04:50,662
Another evolving tool uses a tablet-device camera and colorimetric analysis to calculate blood loss (Spies, 2020; Venkatesh, 2020).

39
00:04:50,662 --> 00:04:56,125
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019b)

40
00:04:56,284 --> 00:05:01,746
recognizes the use of quantitative methods to help identify severe hemorrhage.

41
00:05:01,696 --> 00:05:11,659
Intrapartum, quantitative measurement is more accurate than visual estimation, but the effectiveness of these methods on clinical outcomes has not been demonstrated.

42
00:05:11,659 --> 00:05:23,671
A Cochrane Review of three trials found no differences between subjective and objective methods when comparing outcomes that included need for transfusion, plasma expanders, or uterotonics .

43
00:05:23,671 --> 00:05:28,421
Postpartum, retrospective estimation also can be informative.

44
00:05:28,421 --> 00:05:35,534
The blood volume of a pregnant woman with normal pregnancy-induced hypervolemia usually rises by 50 percent.

45
00:05:35,534 --> 00:05:45,421
However, individual increases range from 30 to 60 percent, that is, 1500 to 2000 mL for an average-sized woman .

46
00:05:45,421 --> 00:05:50,471
The equation to calculate blood volume is shown in Table 42-1.

47
00:05:50,471 --> 00:06:02,196
It is axiomatic that a normal pregnant woman tolerates, without any decrease in postpartum hematocrit, blood loss at delivery that approaches the volume of blood that was added during pregnancy.

48
00:06:02,196 --> 00:06:11,209
Thus, if blood loss is less than the pregnancy-added volume, the hematocrit remains the same acutely and during the first several days postpartum.

49
00:06:11,209 --> 00:06:16,971
It then rises as nonpregnant plasma volume levels return during the next week or so.

50
00:06:16,971 --> 00:06:31,696
Whenever the postpartum hematocrit is lower than one obtained on admission for delivery, blood loss can be estimated as the sum of the calculated pregnancy-added volume plus 500 mL for each 3-volume-percent decline of the hematocrit.

51
00:06:31,696 --> 00:06:34,296
Table 42-1.

52
00:06:34,296 --> 00:06:47,609
Calculation of Maternal Total Blood Volume For research and clinical care initiatives, excessive blood loss has been estimated by several methods (Coviello, 2019; Saoud, 2019).

53
00:06:47,609 --> 00:06:57,946
Tita and colleagues (2012) used a 6-volume-percent drop in the postpartum hematocrit to define clinically significant blood loss with vaginal delivery.

54
00:06:57,946 --> 00:07:04,746
This decline easily signifies a >1000-mL blood loss in the average-sized woman.

55
00:07:04,746 --> 00:07:08,121
They documented this amount in a fourth of women.

56
00:07:08,121 --> 00:07:13,071
Another marker used to estimate hemorrhage incidence is the transfusion rate.

57
00:07:13,071 --> 00:07:20,621
In the study by Tita just cited, more than 6 percent of women who delivered vaginally underwent blood transfusion.

58
00:07:20,621 --> 00:07:30,571
In a study of more than 66,000 women delivered at Parkland Hospital, 2.3 percent overall were given blood transfusions for hypovolemia .

59
00:07:30,571 --> 00:07:34,359
Half of these women had undergone cesarean delivery.

60
00:07:34,359 --> 00:07:43,209
Importantly, for those transfused, these investigators calculated blood loss to average approximately 3500 mL!

61
00:07:43,209 --> 00:07:49,946
The need for blood transfusion is now followed as a severe maternal morbidity (SMM) indicator.

62
00:07:49,946 --> 00:07:55,171
The heightened awareness of SMM is outlined in Chapter 1 (p. 5).

63
00:07:55,171 --> 00:08:00,221
Transfusions represent more than 80 percent of total SMM rates.

64
00:08:00,221 --> 00:08:12,909
The incidence of massive transfusion for postpartum hemorrhage has been reported to be from 25 to 65 per 100,000 births (Green, 2016; Ramler, 2019).

65
00:08:12,909 --> 00:08:19,559
However, defining healthcare quality using transfusion as an SMM metric is problematic.

66
00:08:19,559 --> 00:08:24,609
First, this metric is solely derived from administrative billing codes.

67
00:08:24,609 --> 00:08:29,921
Second, transfusion rates may be skewed based on the hospital’s case-mix .

68
00:08:29,921 --> 00:08:38,821
This can distort values for regional referral centers that care for women at high risk for hemorrhage, such as those with placenta accreta spectrum.

69
00:08:38,821 --> 00:08:41,209
# Risk Factors.

70
00:08:41,209 --> 00:08:47,134
Hemorrhage can manifest at any time during pregnancy, delivery, or the puerperium.

71
00:08:47,134 --> 00:08:53,246
Contributions to maternal death from some of these causes of are shown in Figure 42-2.

72
00:08:53,246 --> 00:09:04,721
The Joint Commission (2019) requires accredited delivery services to employ tools that evaluate maternal hemorrhage risk on admission to labor and delivery and postpartum.

73
00:09:04,721 --> 00:09:18,196
Several methods are available, and the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists’ (2019b) scoring tool stratifies risk using several obstetrical factors listed in Table 42-2.

74
00:09:18,196 --> 00:09:23,259
Unfortunately, these scoring systems only modestly predict hemorrhage .

75
00:09:23,259 --> 00:09:33,996
Kawakita and colleagues (2019) examined three commonly referenced tools and found moderate performance in identifying women at risk during cesarean delivery.

76
00:09:33,996 --> 00:09:43,609
In contrast, the Association of Women’s Health, Obstetric and Neonatal Nurses (AWHONN) risk assessment tool was predictive of those at high risk .

77
00:09:43,780 --> 00:09:46,305
Figure 42-2.

78
00:09:46,255 --> 00:09:51,417
Contributions to maternal death from various causes of obstetrical hemorrhage.

79
00:09:51,417 --> 00:09:56,805
Percentages are approximations because of different classification schemata used.

80
00:09:56,805 --> 00:10:04,167
DIC = disseminated intravascular coagulopathy; PAS = placenta accreta spectrum.

81
00:10:04,167 --> 00:10:12,667
Data from Al-Zirqi, 2008; Berg, 2010; Creanga, 2015; Zwart, 2008.

82
00:10:12,667 --> 00:10:15,205
Table 42-2.

83
00:10:15,205 --> 00:10:22,767
Obstetrical Hemorrhage: Causes, Predisposing Factors, and Vulnerable Patients # Timing of Hemorrhage.

84
00:10:22,767 --> 00:10:36,067
Obstetrical hemorrhage is traditionally classified as antepartum—such as with placenta previa or placental abruption, or as postpartum—commonly caused by uterine atony or genital tract lacerations.

85
00:10:36,067 --> 00:10:45,380
In individual women, however, these terms are nonspecific, and it is reasonable to specify the cause and gestational age as descriptors.

86
00:10:45,380 --> 00:10:49,842
With antepartum hemorrhage, timing may give a clue to its cause.

87
00:10:49,842 --> 00:10:58,080
Many aspects of bleeding during the first half of pregnancy from abortion or ectopic pregnancy are covered in Chapters 11 and 12.

88
00:10:58,080 --> 00:11:02,817
Discussions that follow concern pregnancies with a viable-size fetus.

89
00:11:02,817 --> 00:11:09,792
In these cases, rapid assessment should always consider the deleterious fetal effects of maternal hemorrhage.

90
00:11:09,792 --> 00:11:13,842
During active labor, slight vaginal bleeding is common.

91
00:11:13,842 --> 00:11:20,667
This “bloody show” is the consequence of cervical effacement and dilation and concurrent tearing of small vessels.

92
00:11:20,667 --> 00:11:29,005
However, with uterine bleeding above the cervix, placental abruption, placenta previa, and vasa previa must be considered.

93
00:11:29,005 --> 00:11:36,580
The first two are presented in Chapter 43 and vasa previa is discussed in Chapter 6 (p. 115).

94
00:11:36,580 --> 00:11:44,705
In some women, especially those with a placenta previa, cervical varicosities may bleed (O’Brien, 2013).

95
00:11:44,705 --> 00:11:54,255
Near term in many women, the source of uterine bleeding is not identified, bleeding ceases, and no apparent anatomical cause is found at delivery.

96
00:11:54,255 --> 00:12:00,567
In most of these cases, bleeding likely originated from a slight marginal placental separation.

97
00:12:00,567 --> 00:12:11,405
Despite this, any pregnancy with antepartum bleeding remains at higher risk for an adverse outcome even though bleeding has stopped and placenta previa has been excluded sonographically.

98
00:12:11,405 --> 00:12:16,292
Bleeding after midpregnancy is associated with several adverse outcomes.

99
00:12:16,292 --> 00:12:23,930
The Canadian Perinatal Network described 806 women with hemorrhage between 22 and 28 weeks’ gestation .

100
00:12:23,930 --> 00:12:34,355
Placental abruption (32 percent), previa (21 percent), and cervical bleeding (6.6 percent) were the most frequent causes identified.

101
00:12:34,355 --> 00:12:37,267
In a third, no cause was found.

102
00:12:37,267 --> 00:12:42,605
Of all women, 44 percent were delivered before 29 weeks’ gestation.

103
00:12:42,605 --> 00:12:50,780
In more than 68,000 women in Scotland, the incidence of antepartum hemorrhage after the first trimester was 11 percent .

104
00:12:50,780 --> 00:12:57,917
These women were at significantly higher risk for preterm birth, labor induction, and postpartum hemorrhage.

105
00:12:57,917 --> 00:13:03,292
With postpartum hemorrhage, the source in most cases can and should be determined.

106
00:13:03,292 --> 00:13:09,767
Frequent causes are uterine atony with placental site bleeding, genital tract trauma, or both.

107
00:13:09,767 --> 00:13:12,942
Postpartum hemorrhage is usually obvious.

108
00:13:12,942 --> 00:13:22,767
Important exceptions are unrecognized intrauterine and intravaginal blood accumulation and uterine rupture with intraperitoneal or retroperitoneal bleeding.

109
00:13:22,767 --> 00:13:29,055
Another consideration is an expanding vulvar or vaginal hematoma (p. 740).

110
00:13:29,055 --> 00:13:35,967
Initial evaluation includes attempts to differentiate uterine atony from genital tract lacerations.

111
00:13:35,967 --> 00:13:42,942
For this, risk factors are sought, the lower genital tract is examined, and uterine tone is assessed.

112
00:13:42,942 --> 00:13:52,067
Atony is identified by a boggy, soft uterus during bimanual examination and by expression of clots and hemorrhage during uterine massage.

113
00:13:52,067 --> 00:13:59,605
Persistent bleeding despite a firm, well-contracted uterus suggests that hemorrhage most likely is from lacerations.

114
00:13:59,605 --> 00:14:03,392
Bright red blood further suggests arterial bleeding.

115
00:14:03,392 --> 00:14:11,380
To confirm that lacerations are a source of bleeding, careful inspection of the vagina, cervix, and uterus is essential.

116
00:14:11,380 --> 00:14:15,905
Examination is easier if regional analgesia is employed.

117
00:14:15,905 --> 00:14:21,167
Transfer from a labor and delivery room to an operative suite also may be prudent.

118
00:14:21,167 --> 00:14:31,017
If there are no lower genital tract lacerations and the uterus is contracted, yet supracervical bleeding persists, manual exploration of the uterus

119
00:14:31,180 --> 00:14:34,105
is done to exclude a uterine tear .

120
00:14:34,055 --> 00:14:39,867
This also is completed routinely after internal podalic version or breech extraction.

121
00:14:39,867 --> 00:14:46,005
Some perform this after successful vaginal birth after cesarean, and this is our practice.

122
00:14:46,005 --> 00:14:51,155
Late postpartum hemorrhage describes bleeding after the first 24 hours.

123
00:14:51,155 --> 00:14:57,617
Found in up to 1 percent of women, one risk factor is postpartum hemorrhage at the time of delivery .

124
00:14:57,617 --> 00:15:04,017
Delayed hemorrhage may be serious and is discussed in Chapter 36 (p. 637).

125
00:15:04,017 --> 00:15:07,667
# Appreciation of Estimated Blood Loss.

126
00:15:07,667 --> 00:15:14,305
As discussed, visual blood loss estimates are often inaccurate, especially with excessive bleeding.

127
00:15:14,305 --> 00:15:19,580
Instead of sudden and massive hemorrhage, postpartum bleeding is frequently steady.

128
00:15:19,580 --> 00:15:29,517
If bleeding from atony or laceration persists, it may appear to be only moderate at any given instant but may continue until serious hypovolemia develops.

129
00:15:29,517 --> 00:15:41,555
In some cases, after placental separation, blood may not escape vaginally but instead may collect within the uterine cavity, which can become distended by 1000 mL or more of blood.

130
00:15:41,555 --> 00:15:49,355
Moreover, postpartum uterine massage will be ineffective if applied to a roll of abdominal fat mistaken for the uterus.

131
00:15:49,355 --> 00:15:55,255
All of these factors can lead to an underappreciation of the magnitude of hemorrhage over time.

132
00:15:55,255 --> 00:16:03,680
The effects of hemorrhage depend mainly on the maternal nonpregnant blood volume and the corresponding degree of pregnancy-induced hypervolemia.

133
00:16:03,680 --> 00:16:12,030
Small women—even those with appropriate pregnancyinduced hypervolemia—do not tolerate more than seemingly average blood loss.

134
00:16:12,030 --> 00:16:19,255
Some gravidas may be particularly susceptible to hemorrhage because their blood volume expansion is less than expected.

135
00:16:19,255 --> 00:16:28,905
An example is women with severe preeclampsia or eclampsia, who are more vulnerable to hemorrhage because they frequently do not have a normal blood volume accrual.

136
00:16:28,905 --> 00:16:38,580
Specifically, Zeeman and associates (2009) documented a mean increase above nonpregnant volume of only 10 percent in eclamptic women .

137
00:16:38,580 --> 00:16:46,905
Another example is the moderate to severe curtailing of pregnancy-induced volume expansion in women with chronic renal insufficiency .

138
00:16:46,905 --> 00:16:55,417
When excessive hemorrhage is suspected in these high-risk women, crystalloid and blood are promptly administered for suspected hypovolemia.

139
00:16:55,417 --> 00:17:05,205
A treacherous feature of postpartum hemorrhage is the failure of the pulse and blood pressure to undergo more than moderate alterations until large amounts of blood have been lost.

140
00:17:05,205 --> 00:17:12,917
The normotensive woman initially may actually become somewhat hypertensive from catecholamine release in response to hemorrhage.

141
00:17:12,917 --> 00:17:19,417
Importantly, women with preeclampsia may become “normotensive” despite remarkable hypovolemia.

142
00:17:19,417 --> 00:17:24,342
Accordingly, hypovolemia may not be recognized until very late.

143
00:17:24,342 --> 00:17:26,242
Uterine Atony.

144
00:17:26,242 --> 00:17:29,367
# Third-stage Labor Management.

145
00:17:29,367 --> 00:17:39,955
The most frequent cause of obstetrical hemorrhage is failure of the uterus after delivery to contract sufficiently and arrest bleeding from vessels at the placental implantation site .

146
00:17:39,955 --> 00:17:46,492
That said, some bleeding is inevitable during third-stage labor as the placenta begins to separate.

147
00:17:46,492 --> 00:18:00,605
Blood from the implantation site may escape into the vagina immediately—the Duncan mechanism of placental separation, or it remains concealed behind the placenta and membranes until the placenta is delivered—the Schultze mechanism.

148
00:18:00,605 --> 00:18:04,830
Placental descent is signified by a slack umbilical cord.

149
00:18:04,830 --> 00:18:11,455
After signs of placental separation, the uterus should be massaged if it is not contracted firmly.

150
00:18:11,455 --> 00:18:20,580
Importantly, separation and delivery of the placenta by cord traction, especially when the uterus is atonic, may cause uterine inversion.

151
00:18:20,580 --> 00:18:27,430
Following placental delivery, the fundus is always palpated to confirm that the uterus is well contracted.

152
00:18:27,430 --> 00:18:33,680
If it is not firm, vigorous fundal massage usually prevents postpartum hemorrhage from atony .

153
00:18:33,680 --> 00:18:41,380
Concurrent administration of a uterotonic agent, discussed in the next sections, is another recommended preventive measure.

154
00:18:41,380 --> 00:18:43,767
# Risk Factors.

155
00:18:43,767 --> 00:18:49,080
In many women with known risks, uterine atony can at least be anticipated.

156
00:18:49,080 --> 00:18:56,380
However, as discussed earlier (p. 733), risk-based scoring systems have limited value.

157
00:18:56,380 --> 00:18:59,955
In one study, up to half of women with atony

158
00:19:00,124 --> 00:19:03,886
after cesarean delivery had no risk factors .

159
00:19:03,836 --> 00:19:12,174
The magnitude of risk for atony imposed by each of the factors shown in Table 42-2 varies considerably between reports.

160
00:19:12,174 --> 00:19:15,674
Primiparity and high parity are two factors .

161
00:19:15,674 --> 00:19:25,874
In one study, the incidence of postpartum hemorrhage rose from 0.3 percent in women of low parity to 1.9 percent with parity of four or greater.

162
00:19:25,874 --> 00:19:30,136
It was 2.7 percent with parity of seven or greater .

163
00:19:30,136 --> 00:19:40,424
The overdistended uterus is prone to hypotonia after delivery, and thus women with a large fetus, multiple fetuses, or hydramnios carry greater risk .

164
00:19:40,424 --> 00:19:46,286
Labor abnormalities predispose to atony and include hyper- or hypotonic labor.

165
00:19:46,286 --> 00:19:54,536
Similarly, labor induction or augmentation with either prostaglandins or oxytocin is more likely to be followed by atony .

166
00:19:54,536 --> 00:20:00,449
The frequency of hemorrhage increases with third-stage labor lasting >20 minutes .

167
00:20:00,449 --> 00:20:05,961
Last, the woman who has had a prior postpartum hemorrhage is at risk for recurrence.

168
00:20:05,961 --> 00:20:09,049
# Evaluation and Management.

169
00:20:09,049 --> 00:20:15,561
To improve immediate postpartum care, clinical safety bundles provide a standardized response .

170
00:20:15,561 --> 00:20:23,886
In principle, all of these programs suggest notifying unit personnel, activating resources, and standardizing management.

171
00:20:23,886 --> 00:20:30,349
With immediate postpartum hemorrhage, careful inspection aims to exclude birth canal laceration.

172
00:20:30,349 --> 00:20:38,636
In some cases, bleeding is caused by retained placental fragments, and placental inspection after delivery should be routine.

173
00:20:38,636 --> 00:20:44,399
If a defect is seen, the uterus should be manually explored and the fragment removed.

174
00:20:44,399 --> 00:20:49,899
Occasionally, retention of a succenturiate lobe may cause postpartum hemorrhage .

175
00:20:49,899 --> 00:20:58,274
During examination for lacerations and causes of atony, the uterus is massaged and uterotonic agents are administered.

176
00:20:58,274 --> 00:21:00,911
Figure 42-3.

177
00:21:00,911 --> 00:21:05,499
Parkland Hospital algorithm for management of postpartum hemorrhage.

178
00:21:05,499 --> 00:21:21,186
CBC = complete blood count; EBL = estimated blood loss; INR = international normalized ratio; IV = intravenous; PT = prothrombin time; PTT = partial thromboplastin time.

179
00:21:21,186 --> 00:21:37,074
Manual Removal of the Placenta If heavy bleeding persists after delivery of the newborn but while the placenta remains partially or totally attached, then manual placental removal is indicated (Cummings, 2016; Frolova, 2016).

180
00:21:37,074 --> 00:21:43,361
For this, adequate analgesia is mandatory, and aseptic surgical technique is used.

181
00:21:43,361 --> 00:21:54,524
As illustrated in Figure 27-8 (p. 508), the fingertips of one hand, with fingers approximated, are insinuated between the uterine wall and placenta.

182
00:21:54,524 --> 00:22:00,011
A sweeping forward motion in this plane will peel the placenta off its uterine attachment.

183
00:22:00,011 --> 00:22:07,211
After its removal, trailing membranes are carefully teased free from the decidua using ring forceps as needed.

184
00:22:07,211 --> 00:22:12,761
Another method to clear membranes is to wipe out the uterine cavity with a gauze-wrapped hand.

185
00:22:12,761 --> 00:22:18,749
Bierer forceps guided by ultrasound to remove retained placenta also has been described .

186
00:22:18,749 --> 00:22:30,136
Some administer a single dose of intravenous antibiotics after manual uterine exploration, however, one systematic review of observational studies found no benefits .

187
00:22:30,136 --> 00:22:39,161
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020b) concluded that data neither support nor refute this practice.

188
00:22:39,161 --> 00:22:50,261
The World Health Organization (WHO) (2015) recommends ampicillin or cefazolin antimicrobial prophylaxis after manual placenta removal.

189
00:22:50,261 --> 00:22:57,049
At Parkland Hospital, we routinely provide a single dose of cefazolin after manual exploration.

190
00:22:57,049 --> 00:23:03,411
Uterotonic Agents Several compounds can be used to prompt the postpartum uterus to contract.

191
00:23:03,411 --> 00:23:08,224
One of these is routinely selected and given to prevent postpartum bleeding.

192
00:23:08,224 --> 00:23:19,686
Choices for prophylaxis include oxytocin (Pitocin); the ergots, namely ergonovine (Ergotrate) and methylergonovine (Methergine); or misoprostol (Cytotec) .

193
00:23:19,686 --> 00:23:25,974
The WHO (2018) recommends oxytocin for first-line use for prophylaxis.

194
00:23:25,974 --> 00:23:41,799
For administration, 20 units of oxytocin in 1000 mL of crystalloid solution is effective and given intravenously (IV) at 10 mL/min for a dose of 200 mU/min. Higher concentrations are minimally more efficient .

195
00:23:41,799 --> 00:23:52,524
A summary of oxytocin administration regimens is found in the Practice Brief of the Association of Women’s Health Objective and Neonatal Nurses—AWHONN (2014).

196
00:23:52,524 --> 00:23:54,174
In those

197
00:23:54,340 --> 00:23:59,740
without IV access, oxytocin may be given intramuscularly (IM).

198
00:23:59,690 --> 00:24:08,490
Adnan and colleagues (2018) found the IV route to more effectively prevent severe hemorrhage compared with IM administration.

199
00:24:08,490 --> 00:24:16,327
Oxytocin is never given as an undiluted bolus dose because serious hypotension or cardiac arrhythmias can develop.

200
00:24:16,327 --> 00:24:20,727
Most agents for prevention of atony are also used to treat it.

201
00:24:20,727 --> 00:24:27,015
For atony, IV oxytocin is continued or may be initiated if not selected initially.

202
00:24:27,015 --> 00:24:31,627
It is considered first-line treatment by the WHO (2012).

203
00:24:31,627 --> 00:24:36,990
If bleeding and atony are refractory, an agent from a different group can be added .

204
00:24:36,990 --> 00:24:41,490
Ergot alkaloids have been used for centuries to treat uterine atony.

205
00:24:41,490 --> 00:24:49,365
If atony persists despite oxytocin and other preventive measures, ergot derivatives can be used for second-line treatment.

206
00:24:49,365 --> 00:24:57,852
Given parenterally, these drugs rapidly stimulate tetanic uterine contractions and act for approximately 45 minutes .

207
00:24:57,852 --> 00:25:02,865
A common regimen is 0.2 mg of either drug given IM.

208
00:25:02,865 --> 00:25:07,615
Methergine can be repeated at 2- to 4-hour intervals as needed.

209
00:25:07,615 --> 00:25:16,190
A caveat is that ergot agents, especially given IV, may cause dangerous hypertension, especially in women with preeclampsia.

210
00:25:16,190 --> 00:25:25,802
Severe hypertension is also seen with concomitant use of protease inhibitors given for human immunodeficiency viral (HIV) infection.

211
00:25:25,802 --> 00:25:34,790
These adverse effects notwithstanding, it is speculative whether ergot derivatives offer superior therapeutic effects compared with oxytocin.

212
00:25:34,790 --> 00:25:40,527
Other second-line agents for atony have included the E- and F-series prostaglandins.

213
00:25:40,527 --> 00:25:47,390
Carboprost tromethamine (Hemabate) is the 15-methyl derivative of prostaglandin F2α.

214
00:25:47,390 --> 00:25:55,840
It is approved for uterine atony treatment in a dose of 250 μg (0.25 mg) given IM.

215
00:25:55,840 --> 00:26:03,465
This dose can be repeated if necessary, at 15- to 90-minute intervals up to a maximum of eight doses.

216
00:26:03,465 --> 00:26:08,027
Observational data indicate an 88-percent success rate .

217
00:26:08,027 --> 00:26:13,015
Carboprost causes side effects in approximately 20 percent of women.

218
00:26:13,015 --> 00:26:21,752
These include, in descending order of frequency, diarrhea, hypertension, vomiting, fever, flushing, and tachycardia.

219
00:26:21,752 --> 00:26:27,265
Another pharmacological effect is pulmonary airway and vascular constriction.

220
00:26:27,265 --> 00:26:39,252
Thus, carboprost should not be used for asthmatic women or those with pulmonary hypertension, including women those with suspected amnionic fluid embolism (p. 745).

221
00:26:39,252 --> 00:26:45,477
It has also been reported to cause arterial oxygen desaturation that averaged 10 percent .

222
00:26:45,477 --> 00:26:51,852
We have occasionally encountered severe hypertension with carboprost given to women with preeclampsia.

223
00:26:51,852 --> 00:26:58,315
Other relative contraindications to carboprost include renal, liver, and cardiac disease .

224
00:26:58,315 --> 00:27:02,727
Misoprostol is a synthetic prostaglandin E1 analogue.

225
00:27:02,727 --> 00:27:13,602
In a Cochrane review, Mousa and associates (2014) reported no added benefits for misoprostol use for treatment compared with oxytocin or ergonovine.

226
00:27:13,602 --> 00:27:27,065
If misoprostol is used to treat atony, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019a) recommends a dose of 600 to 1000 µg rectally, orally, or sublingually.

227
00:27:27,065 --> 00:27:32,140
Dinoprostone (Cervidil, Prepidil) is prostaglandin E2.

228
00:27:32,140 --> 00:27:40,952
It may also be used off label for atony treatment and is given as a 20-mg suppository per rectum or per vagina every 2 hours.

229
00:27:40,952 --> 00:27:49,752
It typically causes diarrhea, which is problematic for the rectal route, whereas vigorous vaginal bleeding may preclude its use per vagina.

230
00:27:49,752 --> 00:27:56,177
Hypotension, which is commonly encountered with hemorrhage, is considered a contraindication by some.

231
00:27:56,177 --> 00:28:02,065
For this reason, this agent is not deployed for hemorrhage management at Parkland Hospital.

232
00:28:02,065 --> 00:28:09,215
IV prostaglandin E2—sulprostone—is used in Europe, but it is not available in the United States.

233
00:28:09,215 --> 00:28:15,852
Tranexamic Acid This antifibrinolytic agent has been evaluated to treat postpartum hemorrhage.

234
00:28:15,852 --> 00:28:31,765
In the randomized WOMAN trial of gravidas with hemorrhage following vaginal birth or during cesarean delivery, mortality rates from obstetrical hemorrhage were 1.2 percent in those given a 1-g IV tranexamic acid dose plus traditional care for bleeding.

235
00:28:31,765 --> 00:28:38,702
This rate was significantly lower than the 1.7-percent death rate in women given traditional care alone .

236
00:28:38,702 --> 00:28:44,440
Another study of women with hemorrhage following vaginal birth found that rates of progression to

237
00:28:44,596 --> 00:28:53,321
severe PPH, of transfusion, and of peripartum hysterectomy were lower in the TXA group compared with the traditional-care group .

238
00:28:53,271 --> 00:29:00,508
Use of tranexamic acid in hemorrhage is discussed further in Chapter 44 (p. 773).

239
00:29:00,508 --> 00:29:18,246
Bleeding Unresponsive to Uterotonic Agents If bleeding persists after initial measures for atony, the following management steps are performed immediately and simultaneously: 1. Begin bimanual uterine compression, which is easily done and controls most cases of continuing hemorrhage .

240
00:29:18,246 --> 00:29:21,683
This technique is not simply fundal massage.

241
00:29:21,683 --> 00:29:30,008
The posterior uterine wall is massaged by one hand on the abdomen, while the other hand is made into a fist and placed into the vagina.

242
00:29:30,008 --> 00:29:38,108
This fist kneads the anterior uterine wall through the anterior vaginal wall, and the uterus is compressed between the two hands.

243
00:29:38,108 --> 00:29:40,758
Figure 42-4.

244
00:29:40,758 --> 00:29:43,933
Bimanual compression for uterine atony.

245
00:29:43,933 --> 00:29:53,646
The uterus is positioned with the fist of one hand in the anterior fornix pushing against the anterior wall, which is held in place by the other hand on the abdomen.

246
00:29:53,646 --> 00:29:57,583
The abdominal hand is also used for uterine massage.

247
00:29:57,583 --> 00:30:03,908
2. Mobilize the emergent-care obstetrical team and call for whole blood or packed red cells.

248
00:30:03,908 --> 00:30:07,721
3. Request urgent help from the anesthesia team.

249
00:30:07,721 --> 00:30:16,658
4. Secure at least two large-bore IV catheters so that crystalloid with oxytocin can be continued simultaneously with blood products.

250
00:30:16,658 --> 00:30:21,296
Insert a Foley catheter for continuous urine output monitoring.

251
00:30:21,296 --> 00:30:28,346
5. Begin volume resuscitation with rapid IV crystalloid infusion (p. 734).

252
00:30:28,346 --> 00:30:44,071
6. With sedation, analgesia, or anesthesia established and now with optimal exposure, once again manually explore the uterine cavity for retained placental fragments and for uterine abnormalities, including lacerations or rupture.

253
00:30:44,071 --> 00:30:50,871
7. Thoroughly inspect the cervix and vagina again for lacerations that may have escaped attention.

254
00:30:50,871 --> 00:30:56,883
8. Administer blood transfusions if the woman is still unstable or if bleeding persists .

255
00:30:56,883 --> 00:31:06,846
At this juncture, after causes other than atony have been excluded and after hypovolemia is reversed, several other measures are considered if bleeding continues .

256
00:31:06,846 --> 00:31:14,221
Their use depends on several factors such as parity, desire for sterilization, and experience with each method.

257
00:31:14,221 --> 00:31:17,533
Balloon Tamponade and Surgical Procedures.

258
00:31:17,533 --> 00:31:28,546
For this, the tip of a 24F to 30F Foley catheter with a 30-mL balloon is guided into the uterine cavity and filled with 60 to 80 mL of saline.

259
00:31:28,546 --> 00:31:33,071
The open tip permits continuous drainage of blood from the uterus.

260
00:31:33,071 --> 00:31:43,046
We have experienced balloon rupture when more than 50 mL was instilled into the balloon, thus a 34F Foley with a 60-ml balloon can be used.

261
00:31:43,046 --> 00:31:49,083
If bleeding subsides, the catheter is typically removed after 12 to 24 hours.

262
00:31:49,083 --> 00:32:00,421
Similar devices for tamponade include SengstakenBlakemore, Rusch, and ebb balloons and condom catheters (Antony, 2017; Kondoh, 2019).

263
00:32:00,421 --> 00:32:11,108
Antibiotic prophylaxis using cefazolin, 1 gram every 8 hours until removal, has recently been suggested to reduce risk of postpartum endometritis .

264
00:32:11,108 --> 00:32:18,971
Instead, specially constructed intrauterine balloons are available to treat hemorrhage from uterine atony and other causes.

265
00:32:18,971 --> 00:32:27,058
A Bakri Postpartum Balloon or BT-Cath may be inserted and inflated to tamponade the endometrial cavity and stop bleeding .

266
00:32:27,058 --> 00:32:30,596
Insertion requires two or three team members.

267
00:32:30,596 --> 00:32:34,821
The first performs abdominal sonography during the procedure.

268
00:32:34,821 --> 00:32:39,858
The second places the deflated balloon into the uterus and stabilizes it.

269
00:32:39,858 --> 00:32:53,521
The third member instills fluid to inflate the balloon, rapidly infusing at least 150 mL followed by further instillation over a few minutes for a total of 300 to 500 mL to arrest hemorrhage.

270
00:32:53,521 --> 00:32:58,346
It is reasonable to remove the balloon after approximately 12 hours .

271
00:32:58,346 --> 00:33:01,058
Figure 42-5.

272
00:33:01,058 --> 00:33:04,971
Intrauterine Bakri balloon for postpartum hemorrhage.

273
00:33:04,971 --> 00:33:18,471
In small studies evaluating uterine balloons for all causes, the success rate approximated 85 percent (Kaya, 2016; Said Ali, 2021; Vintejoux, 2015).

274
00:33:18,471 --> 00:33:28,108
From their review, Suarez and associates (2020) used balloon tamponade in 4729 women and reported a similar success rate.

275
00:33:28,108 --> 00:33:32,608
At least one case of uterine rupture with a balloon has been reported .

276
00:33:32,608 --> 00:33:36,021
Combinations of balloon tamponade and uterine

277
00:33:36,172 --> 00:33:43,009
compression sutures also have been described (Diemert, 2012; Yoong, 2012).

278
00:33:42,959 --> 00:33:48,247
Failure of uterotonic agents and tamponade requires more invasive methods.

279
00:33:48,247 --> 00:33:56,409
These include uterine compression sutures, major pelvic vessel ligation, angiographic embolization, and hysterectomy.

280
00:33:56,409 --> 00:34:01,872
These are discussed in detail in Chapters 44 (p. 778).

281
00:34:01,872 --> 00:34:07,409
Peripartum hysterectomy is illustrated in Chapter 30 (p. 560).

282
00:34:07,409 --> 00:34:09,447
Uterine Inversion.

283
00:34:09,447 --> 00:34:15,884
Puerperal inversion of the uterus is one of the classic hemorrhagic disasters encountered in obstetrics.

284
00:34:15,884 --> 00:34:22,122
Unless promptly recognized and managed appropriately, associated bleeding often is massive.

285
00:34:22,122 --> 00:34:39,647
Risk factors include alone or in combination: (1) placental implantation at the fundus, (2) uterine atony, (3) cord traction applied before placental separation, and (4) abnormally adherent placentation seen with placenta accreta spectrum disorders .

286
00:34:39,647 --> 00:34:48,172
Other risk factors are a short cord, uterine wall weakening at the implantation site, uterine tumors, and excessive fundal pressure .

287
00:34:48,172 --> 00:34:56,172
Depending on which of these factors are contributory, the incidence and severity of uterine inversion varies and can be progressive .

288
00:34:56,172 --> 00:35:01,547
The worst scenario is complete inversion with the uterus protruding from the birth canal .

289
00:35:01,547 --> 00:35:04,222
Figure 42-6.

290
00:35:04,222 --> 00:35:07,309
Progressive degrees of uterine inversion.

291
00:35:07,309 --> 00:35:09,922
Figure 42-7.

292
00:35:09,922 --> 00:35:18,697
Maternal death during home delivery caused by exsanguination from uterine inversion, which stemmed from a fundally implanted placenta increta.

293
00:35:18,697 --> 00:35:29,672
The incidence of uterine inversion ranges from 1 case in 2000 to 1 in 20,000 vaginal deliveries (Coad, 2017; Witteveen, 2013).

294
00:35:29,672 --> 00:35:36,047
Our experiences at Parkland Hospital correspond with the higher 1:2000 incidence.

295
00:35:36,047 --> 00:35:43,972
This is despite our policy of discouraging placental delivery by cord traction alone, and before certainty of its separation.

296
00:35:43,972 --> 00:35:58,559
It is unknown if active management of third-stage labor with cord traction applied ostensibly after signs of placental separation raises the likelihood of uterine inversion (DeneuxTharaux, 2013; Prick, 2013).

297
00:35:58,559 --> 00:36:01,522
# Recognition and Management.

298
00:36:01,522 --> 00:36:07,997
Immediate recognition of uterine inversion improves the chances of a quick resolution and good outcome.

299
00:36:07,997 --> 00:36:14,784
If initially unrecognized, continued hemorrhage likely will prompt closer examination of the birth canal.

300
00:36:14,784 --> 00:36:28,872
Although complete inversion is usually evident, the partially inverted uterus can be mistaken for a uterine myoma, and sonography can aid differentiation (Pan, 2015; Smulian, 2013).

301
00:36:28,872 --> 00:36:35,597
Many cases are associated with immediate life-threatening hemorrhage, and a fourth require blood replacement .

302
00:36:35,597 --> 00:36:48,609
Once any degree of uterine inversion is recognized, several steps must be implemented urgently and simultaneously: 1. Immediate assistance is summoned, including obstetrical and anesthesia personnel.

303
00:36:48,609 --> 00:36:52,884
2. Blood is brought to the delivery suite for potential use.

304
00:36:52,884 --> 00:37:02,609
3. Two large-bore IV infusion systems are secured to begin rapid crystalloid infusion to treat hypovolemia while awaiting arrival of blood products.

305
00:37:02,609 --> 00:37:07,397
4. The woman is evaluated for emergency general anesthesia.

306
00:37:07,397 --> 00:37:24,372
5. If the recently inverted uterus has not contracted and retracted completely and if the placenta has already separated, the uterus may often be replaced simply by pushing up on the inverted fundus with the palm of the hand and fingers in the direction of the long axis of the vagina .

307
00:37:24,372 --> 00:37:29,622
Some use two fingers rigidly extended to push the center of the fundus upward.

308
00:37:29,622 --> 00:37:35,409
Care is taken not to apply so much pressure as to perforate the uterus with the fingertips.

309
00:37:35,409 --> 00:37:37,859
Figure 42-8.

310
00:37:37,859 --> 00:37:48,647
Incomplete uterine inversion can be repositioned by using the abdominal hand for palpation of the crater-like depression while simultaneously gently pushing the inverted fundus upward.

311
00:37:48,647 --> 00:37:55,797
6. If the placenta is still attached, attempts are made to reposition the uterus with the placenta in situ.

312
00:37:55,797 --> 00:38:03,109
In theory, the uterus contracts to a lesser degree and placenta-site blood loss is less with an attached placenta.

313
00:38:03,109 --> 00:38:10,622
At this time a trial of an intravenously administered tocolytic drug may aid uterine relaxation and repositioning.

314
00:38:10,622 --> 00:38:21,409
Of options, a 250-µg subcutaneous dose of terbutaline, a 50- to 100-µg IV dose of nitroglycerin, or a 4-g IV dose of magnesium

315
00:38:21,580 --> 00:38:27,880
sulfate are suitable (Catanzarite, 1986; Dufour, 1997).

316
00:38:27,830 --> 00:38:36,742
If these fail to provide sufficient relaxation, then a rapid-acting halogenated inhalational agent is administered by anesthesia staff.

317
00:38:36,742 --> 00:38:41,880
After the uterus is replaced, the placenta is carefully manually removed.

318
00:38:41,880 --> 00:38:50,442
7. If uterine repositioning fails with the placenta attached, it is peeled off, and uterine repositioning is attempted again.

319
00:38:50,442 --> 00:39:04,055
8. Once the uterus is restored to its normal configuration, tocolysis is stopped, oxytocin is then infused, and other uterotonic agents may be given as described for atony (p. 735).

320
00:39:04,055 --> 00:39:16,967
Meanwhile, the operator maintains the fundus in its normal anatomical position while applying bimanual compression to control further hemorrhage until the uterus is well contracted (see Fig. 42-4).

321
00:39:16,967 --> 00:39:23,480
The operator continues to monitor the uterus transvaginally for evidence of subsequent inversion.

322
00:39:23,480 --> 00:39:32,255
A Bakri balloon has been used by some to maintain the repositioned uterus (Haeri, 2015; Ida, 2015).

323
00:39:32,255 --> 00:39:35,055
# Surgical Intervention.

324
00:39:35,055 --> 00:39:41,867
In most cases, the inverted uterus can be restored to its normal position by the just-described techniques.

325
00:39:41,867 --> 00:39:45,380
Occasionally, manual replacement fails.

326
00:39:45,380 --> 00:39:49,305
One cause is a dense myometrial constriction ring.

327
00:39:49,305 --> 00:39:52,767
At this point, laparotomy is imperative.

328
00:39:52,767 --> 00:39:59,455
The anatomical configuration found at surgery can be confusing as shown in Figure 42-9.

329
00:39:59,455 --> 00:40:09,980
During laparotomy and after tocolysis is given, a combined effort is made to reposition the uterus by concurrently pushing upward from below and pulling upward from above.

330
00:40:09,980 --> 00:40:17,405
Application of atraumatic clamps to each round ligament and upward traction may be helpful —the Huntington procedure.

331
00:40:17,405 --> 00:40:26,405
In some cases, placing a deep traction suture in the inverted fundus or grasping it with tissue forceps may help but can be technically difficult.

332
00:40:26,405 --> 00:40:36,617
If a constriction ring still prohibits repositioning, a sagittal surgical cut—Haultain incision—is made posteriorly through the muscular ring to release it.

333
00:40:36,617 --> 00:40:39,930
The exposed fundus can then be reinverted .

334
00:40:39,930 --> 00:40:42,580
Figure 42-9.

335
00:40:42,580 --> 00:40:47,992
Surgical anatomy of a completely inverted uterus viewed from above at laparotomy.

336
00:40:47,992 --> 00:40:55,305
After uterine replacement, tocolytics are stopped, and oxytocin and other uterotonic agents are given.

337
00:40:55,305 --> 00:40:59,742
If the Haultain method is used, the uterine incision is repaired.

338
00:40:59,742 --> 00:41:08,055
Risks of separation of this posterior hysterotomy incision during subsequent pregnancy, labor, and delivery are unknown .

339
00:41:08,055 --> 00:41:14,955
Further illustration and discussion are found in Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd edition .

340
00:41:14,955 --> 00:41:20,842
In some cases, the uterus will again invert almost immediately after repositioning.

341
00:41:20,842 --> 00:41:30,305
With this problem, uterine compression sutures can be used to prevent another inversion (Matsubara, 2009; Mondal, 2012).

342
00:41:30,305 --> 00:41:36,230
Occasionally, chronic puerperal uterine inversion may become apparent weeks after delivery.

343
00:41:36,230 --> 00:41:38,492
Birth Canal Injuries.

344
00:41:38,492 --> 00:41:41,717
# Vulvovaginal Lacerations.

345
00:41:41,717 --> 00:41:48,480
Childbirth is invariably associated with trauma to the uterus and cervix, vagina, and perineum.

346
00:41:48,480 --> 00:41:55,367
Injuries range from minor mucosal tears to lacerations that create life-threatening hemorrhage or hematomas.

347
00:41:55,367 --> 00:42:05,942
According to the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020a), up to 80 percent of women sustain some type of laceration at vaginal birth.

348
00:42:05,942 --> 00:42:10,605
These may lie proximally or distally along the lower genital tract.

349
00:42:10,605 --> 00:42:15,405
Small tears of the vaginal wall near the urethra are relatively common.

350
00:42:15,405 --> 00:42:23,092
They are often superficial with little to no bleeding but occasionally require fine-gauge absorbable sutures for hemostasis.

351
00:42:23,092 --> 00:42:32,867
Those large enough to require extensive repair are typically associated with short-term voiding difficulty, and an indwelling bladder catheter will obviate this.

352
00:42:32,867 --> 00:42:40,080
Deeper perineal lacerations are usually accompanied by varying degrees of injury to the outer third of the vagina.

353
00:42:40,080 --> 00:42:45,442
Some extend to involve the anal sphincter or varying depths of the vaginal walls.

354
00:42:45,442 --> 00:42:51,605
Repair of these perineal lacerations is detailed in Chapter 27 (p. 510).

355
00:42:51,605 --> 00:42:59,342
Lacerations involving the middle or upper third of the vagina usually are comorbid with injuries of the perineum or cervix.

356
00:42:59,342 --> 00:43:03,080
These sometimes are missed unless inspection is thorough.

357
00:43:03,080 --> 00:43:06,142
Those that extend upward usually are

358
00:43:06,292 --> 00:43:08,204
longitudinal.

359
00:43:08,154 --> 00:43:15,379
They may follow spontaneous delivery but frequently result from injuries sustained during operative vaginal delivery.

360
00:43:15,379 --> 00:43:23,104
Most involve deeper underlying tissues and thus usually cause significant hemorrhage, which is controlled by suture repair.

361
00:43:23,104 --> 00:43:33,554
For this, effective analgesia or anesthesia, clear visualization, capable assistance, and sufficient resuscitation of hypovolemia are mandatory.

362
00:43:33,554 --> 00:43:42,767
Extensive vaginal or cervical tears should prompt a careful search for evidence of retroperitoneal hemorrhage or of peritoneal perforation with hemorrhage.

363
00:43:42,767 --> 00:43:48,354
Intrauterine exploration is also considered to exclude uterine tears or rupture .

364
00:43:48,354 --> 00:43:55,004
If peritoneal perforation or uterine rupture is strongly suspected, laparotomy is reasonable .

365
00:43:55,004 --> 00:44:04,167
As discussed later (p. 742), imaging and potential embolization may be suitable for large retroperitoneal hematomas.

366
00:44:04,167 --> 00:44:07,142
# Cervical Lacerations.

367
00:44:07,142 --> 00:44:14,392
Superficial lacerations of the cervix can be seen on close inspection in more than half of all vaginal deliveries.

368
00:44:14,392 --> 00:44:20,167
Most of these measure <0.5 cm and seldom require repair.

369
00:44:20,167 --> 00:44:25,167
Deeper lacerations are less frequent, but even these may be unnoticed.

370
00:44:25,167 --> 00:44:31,667
Such lacerations are more likely to be associated with vacuum- or forceps-assisted vaginal delivery .

371
00:44:31,667 --> 00:44:36,417
Due to ascertainment bias, variable incidences are described.

372
00:44:36,417 --> 00:44:48,467
For example, with close inspection, the incidence of cervical lacerations in the Consortium on Safe Labor database was 1 percent in nulliparas and 0.5 percent in multiparas .

373
00:44:48,467 --> 00:44:56,517
But, the overall incidence in a study of more than 81,000 Israeli women was only 0.16 percent .

374
00:44:56,517 --> 00:45:03,117
Cervical lacerations are not usually problematic unless they cause hemorrhage or extend to the vagina.

375
00:45:03,117 --> 00:45:13,729
Rarely, the cervix may be entirely or partially avulsed from the vagina in the anterior, posterior, or lateral fornices, an injury termed colporrhexis.

376
00:45:13,729 --> 00:45:21,217
Another rare injury is avulsion of the entire vaginal portion of the cervix—annular or circular detachment.

377
00:45:21,217 --> 00:45:29,854
These injuries sometimes follow forceps deliveries performed through an incompletely dilated cervix and with the blades applied against the cervix.

378
00:45:29,854 --> 00:45:37,579
In some women, cervical tears reach into the lower uterine segment and involve the uterine artery and its major branches.

379
00:45:37,579 --> 00:45:41,354
They occasionally extend into the peritoneal cavity.

380
00:45:41,354 --> 00:45:50,117
More severe lacerations usually manifest as external hemorrhage or as a hematoma, however, they may occasionally be unsuspected.

381
00:45:50,117 --> 00:45:58,054
In the Israeli study just cited, almost 11 percent of women with a cervical laceration required blood transfusions .

382
00:45:58,054 --> 00:46:05,542
At times, the edematous anterior cervical lip is compressed between the fetal head and maternal symphysis pubis.

383
00:46:05,542 --> 00:46:10,154
This usually is of little consequence and resolves spontaneously.

384
00:46:10,154 --> 00:46:18,779
Rarely, this causes severe ischemia, and the anterior lip may undergo necrosis and later separate from the rest of the cervix.

385
00:46:18,779 --> 00:46:29,329
As with vulvovaginal lacerations, cervical tears can be more fully appreciated with adequate exposure, which may be best attained with transfer to an operating room.

386
00:46:29,329 --> 00:46:38,229
An assistant applies firm downward pressure on the uterus, while the operator exerts gentle traction on the lips of the cervix with ring forceps.

387
00:46:38,229 --> 00:46:46,142
A second assistant can provide even better exposure with right-angle vaginal wall retractors or Breisky vaginal retractors.

388
00:46:46,142 --> 00:46:50,117
Yankauer or other suction tips also can aid viewing.

389
00:46:50,117 --> 00:46:56,642
In general, cervical lacerations of 1 to 2 cm are not repaired unless they are bleeding.

390
00:46:56,642 --> 00:47:07,567
Such tears heal rapidly and ultimately form an irregular, sometimes stellate-appearing, external cervical os (Fig. 36-1, p. 635).

391
00:47:07,567 --> 00:47:11,617
Deep cervical tears usually require surgical repair.

392
00:47:11,617 --> 00:47:25,042
When the laceration is limited to the cervix or even when it extends somewhat into the vaginal fornix, satisfactory results are obtained by suturing the cervix after bringing it into view as depicted in Figure 42-10.

393
00:47:25,042 --> 00:47:34,929
While cervical lacerations are repaired, any associated vaginal lacerations or an episiotomy may be tamponaded with gauze packs to arrest their bleeding.

394
00:47:34,929 --> 00:47:44,542
Because hemorrhage usually comes from the upper angle of the wound, the first suture using 2–0 chromic or polyglactin is placed in tissue above the angle.

395
00:47:44,692 --> 00:47:51,779
Subsequently, either interrupted or continuous locking sutures are serially placed outward toward the operator.

396
00:47:51,729 --> 00:48:04,317
If the uterus is involved and hemorrhage persists, some of the surgical and angiographic methods described in Chapter 44 (p. 777) may be necessary to obtain hemostasis.

397
00:48:04,317 --> 00:48:17,654
Following cervical lacerations, subsequent pregnancy outcomes include excessive recurrent lacerations, cervical incompetence, preterm labor, cesarean delivery, and severe perineal lacerations .

398
00:48:17,654 --> 00:48:20,229
Figure 42-10.

399
00:48:20,229 --> 00:48:24,792
Repair of cervical laceration with appropriate surgical exposure.

400
00:48:24,792 --> 00:48:30,404
Continuous absorbable sutures are placed beginning at the upper angle of the laceration.

401
00:48:30,404 --> 00:48:33,242
# Puerperal Hematomas.

402
00:48:33,242 --> 00:48:38,879
Pelvic hematomas can have several anatomical manifestations following childbirth.

403
00:48:38,879 --> 00:48:47,404
One classification is anatomical and describes vulvar, vulvovaginal, paravaginal, and retroperitoneal hematomas.

404
00:48:47,404 --> 00:48:56,979
Vulvar hematomas may involve the vestibular bulb or branches of the pudendal artery, which are the inferior rectal, perineal, and clitoral arteries .

405
00:48:56,979 --> 00:49:02,129
Paravaginal hematomas may involve the descending branch of the uterine artery.

406
00:49:02,129 --> 00:49:09,279
In some cases, a torn vessel lies above the pelvic fascia, and a supralevator hematoma develops.

407
00:49:09,279 --> 00:49:15,179
These can extend into the upper portion of the vaginal canal and may almost occlude its lumen.

408
00:49:15,179 --> 00:49:21,654
Continued bleeding may dissect retroperitoneally to form a mass palpable above the inguinal ligament.

409
00:49:21,654 --> 00:49:27,792
In some cases, it may even dissect up behind the ascending colon to the hepatic flexure .

410
00:49:27,792 --> 00:49:30,429
Figure 42-11.

411
00:49:30,429 --> 00:49:34,479
Schematic drawing showing types of puerperal hematomas.

412
00:49:34,479 --> 00:49:38,392
A. Coronal view showing a supralevator hematoma.

413
00:49:38,392 --> 00:49:43,179
B. Coronal view showing a hematoma in the urogenital triangle.

414
00:49:43,179 --> 00:49:49,192
C. Perineal view showing anal triangle anatomy and an ischioanal fossa hematoma.

415
00:49:49,192 --> 00:49:55,692
Reproduced with permission from Cunningham FG: Genital tract lacerations and hematomas.

416
00:49:55,692 --> 00:50:05,004
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al (eds): Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

417
00:50:05,004 --> 00:50:09,392
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017a.

418
00:50:09,392 --> 00:50:17,554
Risks for puerperal hematomas include vaginal or perineal laceration, episiotomy, or operative vaginal delivery .

419
00:50:17,554 --> 00:50:28,129
Any hematoma can also develop following stretch and rupture of a blood vessel without an associated laceration (Alturki, 2018; Lee, 2020).

420
00:50:28,129 --> 00:50:31,729
This may be especially true with forceps delivery.

421
00:50:31,729 --> 00:50:36,692
Occasionally, they are associated with an underlying coagulopathy .

422
00:50:36,692 --> 00:50:45,092
Diagnosis Perineal, vulvar, and paravaginal hematomas can develop rapidly and frequently cause excruciating pain .

423
00:50:45,092 --> 00:50:55,654
A tense, tender swelling of varying size rapidly develops, encroaches on the vaginal lumen, and causes overlying skin or epithelium to become ecchymotic.

424
00:50:55,654 --> 00:50:59,879
A paravaginal hematoma may escape detection initially.

425
00:50:59,879 --> 00:51:06,454
However, symptoms of pelvic pressure, pain, or inability to void should prompt evaluation.

426
00:51:06,454 --> 00:51:11,667
Others may go undetected until other measures of hypovolemia become evident.

427
00:51:11,667 --> 00:51:19,867
With a supralevator extension, the hematoma extends upward in the paravaginal space and between the leaves of the broad ligament.

428
00:51:19,867 --> 00:51:27,092
The hematoma may escape detection until it can be felt on abdominal palpation or until hypovolemia develops.

429
00:51:27,092 --> 00:51:36,792
Imaging with sonography or computed tomographic scanning may be useful (Cichowski, 2017; Kawamura, 2014).

430
00:51:36,792 --> 00:51:39,417
Figure 42-12.

431
00:51:39,417 --> 00:51:43,179
Right vulvar hematoma that required evacuation.

432
00:51:43,179 --> 00:51:46,717
A Foley catheter was placed for bladder drainage.

433
00:51:46,717 --> 00:51:51,154
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Emily H. Adhikari.

434
00:51:51,154 --> 00:51:56,642
Management Small hematomas often remain contained and show minimal expansion.

435
00:51:56,642 --> 00:52:02,967
In others, the tissues overlying an expanding hematoma may rupture from pressure necrosis.

436
00:52:02,967 --> 00:52:11,292
In some, profuse hemorrhage may follow, but in other cases, the hematoma drains in the form of large clots and old blood.

437
00:52:11,292 --> 00:52:20,142
In those that involve the paravaginal space and extend above the levator sling, retroperitoneal bleeding may be massive and occasionally fatal.

438
00:52:20,142 --> 00:52:26,217
In rare instances, we encountered hematomas that rebled up to 2 weeks postpartum .

439
00:52:26,217 --> 00:52:34,154
Vulvovaginal hematomas are managed according to their size, location, duration since delivery, and expansion.

440
00:52:34,154 --> 00:52:40,992
If bleeding ceases, then small- to moderate-sized hematomas may be treated expectantly until absorbed.

441
00:52:40,992 --> 00:52:42,417
Cool

442
00:52:42,580 --> 00:52:48,942
packs and analgesics are supportive care, and an inability to void merits catheter drainage.

443
00:52:48,892 --> 00:52:56,080
But, if pain is severe or if the hematoma continues to enlarge, surgical exploration is preferable.

444
00:52:56,080 --> 00:53:02,667
Blood loss with large puerperal hematomas is nearly always considerably more than the clinical estimate.

445
00:53:02,667 --> 00:53:09,555
Hypovolemia is common, and transfusions are frequently required when surgical repair is necessary.

446
00:53:09,555 --> 00:53:17,867
For repair, an incision is made at the point of maximal distention, blood and clots are evacuated, and bleeding points ligated.

447
00:53:17,867 --> 00:53:21,842
Often, no distinct bleeding vessels are identified.

448
00:53:21,842 --> 00:53:26,530
The cavity may then be obliterated in layers with absorbable sutures.

449
00:53:26,530 --> 00:53:31,392
With this, layered tissue closure acts to tamponade low-pressure bleeding.

450
00:53:31,392 --> 00:53:40,467
With vaginal hematomas, the vagina is packed with 1 to 2 gauze roll(s) for 12 to 24 hours, and the number of rolls is recorded.

451
00:53:40,467 --> 00:53:44,367
Supralevator hematomas are more difficult to treat.

452
00:53:44,367 --> 00:53:54,342
Although some can be evacuated by vulvar or vaginal incisions, laparotomy or angiographic embolization are considerations if bleeding continues.

453
00:53:54,342 --> 00:53:59,605
Embolization has become popular for management of some puerperal hematomas.

454
00:53:59,605 --> 00:54:04,855
This is especially true for supralevator or retroperitoneal hematomas.

455
00:54:04,855 --> 00:54:21,492
Embolization can be used primarily, or more likely secondarily, if surgical attempts at hemostasis have failed or if the hematoma is difficult to access surgically (Distefano, 2013; Lee, 2012; Poujade, 2012).

456
00:54:21,492 --> 00:54:30,967
The use of a Bakri balloon for a paracervical hematoma also has been described (Gizzo, 2013; Grönvall, 2014).

457
00:54:30,967 --> 00:54:37,067
Last, ultrasound-guided drainage of a recurrent supralevator hematoma has been reported .

458
00:54:37,067 --> 00:54:38,967
Uterine Rupture.

459
00:54:38,967 --> 00:54:41,930
# Predisposing Factors.

460
00:54:41,930 --> 00:54:47,392
Uterine rupture is another event that may lead to potentially catastrophic hemorrhage.

461
00:54:47,392 --> 00:54:53,667
Rupture may be primary, defined as occurring in a previously intact or unscarred uterus.

462
00:54:53,667 --> 00:55:01,592
More often, rupture is secondary and associated with a preexisting incision, anomaly, or injury of the myometrium .

463
00:55:01,592 --> 00:55:04,180
Table 42-3.

464
00:55:04,180 --> 00:55:11,680
Some Causes of Uterine Rupture The contribution of each of these underlying causes has evolved during the past 50 years.

465
00:55:11,680 --> 00:55:20,655
Before 1960, when cesarean delivery was infrequent and women of great parity were numerous, primary uterine rupture predominated.

466
00:55:20,655 --> 00:55:39,980
As the incidence of cesarean delivery rose, and especially as a subsequent trial of labor in these women became prevalent through the 1990s, uterine rupture through the cesarean hysterotomy scar became the preeminent cause (Chang, 2020; Gibbins, 2015; Mone, 2016).

467
00:55:39,980 --> 00:55:49,930
Concurrent with the diminished enthusiasm for a trial of labor after cesarean delivery (TOLAC), incidence trends for the two types of rupture have again changed.

468
00:55:49,930 --> 00:56:00,692
In a study of 3942 cases of uterine rupture in more than 15 million women, only approximately half were in women with a prior cesarean delivery .

469
00:56:00,692 --> 00:56:11,242
The International Network of Obstetric Survey Systems (INOSS) identified 864 complete uterine ruptures in more than 2.6 million deliveries .

470
00:56:11,242 --> 00:56:15,755
The overall prevalence was 3 cases per 10,000 births.

471
00:56:15,755 --> 00:56:27,030
In women without a prior cesarean delivery, the prevalence was 0.6 per 10,000 births, and it was 22 per 10,000 in those with a prior cesarean delivery.

472
00:56:27,030 --> 00:56:33,505
This is similar to the recent overall rate of 5 cases per 10,000 births at Parkland Hospital.

473
00:56:33,505 --> 00:56:44,517
During this time, there were 40 cases of uterine rupture, and despite a very conservative TOLAC policy, 25 of the 40 events were in women with a prior hysterotomy.

474
00:56:44,517 --> 00:56:50,392
The current lower incidence of women of great parity also has lowered the primary-rupture rate.

475
00:56:50,392 --> 00:56:55,305
In this regard, advancing maternal age may be a greater risk factor .

476
00:56:55,305 --> 00:57:00,630
Another risk is excessive or inappropriate uterine stimulation with oxytocin.

477
00:57:00,630 --> 00:57:04,605
This previously frequent cause has mostly disappeared.

478
00:57:04,605 --> 00:57:17,180
In an analysis of three trials comparing contemporaneous high- and low-dose oxytocin regimens for labor induction in those with an unscarred uterus, the rate of uterine rupture did not differ between groups .

479
00:57:17,180 --> 00:57:27,605
The rate of rupture, however, is increased with sequential induction of labor with prostaglandins and oxytocin in those both with and without prior cesarean delivery .

480
00:57:27,605 --> 00:57:30,580
Other operations that traumatize the

481
00:57:30,748 --> 00:57:44,498
myometrium include uterine curettage or perforation, endometrial ablation, myomectomy, operative hysteroscopy, or prior uterine rupture (Frank, 2018; Zhao, 2019).

482
00:57:44,448 --> 00:57:54,148
Uterine rupture is also linked with disorders associated with connective tissue weakness (Cauldwell, 2019a,b; Noh, 2013).

483
00:57:54,148 --> 00:57:59,785
Another report described rupture in a woman with prior childhood pelvic radiotherapy .

484
00:57:59,785 --> 00:58:04,810
Blunt abdominal trauma also can rupture a substantially gravid uterus .

485
00:58:04,810 --> 00:58:13,385
Thus, pregnant women in the later second- and third trimesters who sustain such trauma are ideally monitored for signs of uterine rupture.

486
00:58:13,385 --> 00:58:19,435
Specific monitoring protocols are outlined in Chapter 50 (p. 896).

487
00:58:19,435 --> 00:58:31,423
Uncommon causes today are those due to internal podalic version and extraction, difficult forceps delivery, breech extraction, and unusual fetal enlargement such as with hydrocephalus.

488
00:58:31,423 --> 00:58:38,460
In one unusual case, fundal rupture followed spontaneous labor in a woman with an abdominal cerclage .

489
00:58:38,460 --> 00:58:50,448
Rupture associated with multifetal pregnancy or uterine anomalies also is rare (Bankada, 2015; Tarney, 2013; Tola, 2014).

490
00:58:50,448 --> 00:58:58,085
Although rare, rudimentary uterine horns are especially susceptible and described in Chapter 3 (p. 43).

491
00:58:58,085 --> 00:59:06,223
Discussed in Chapter 43 (p. 760), placenta percreta may cause focal myometrial weakness and rupture .

492
00:59:06,223 --> 00:59:08,773
# Pathogenesis.

493
00:59:08,773 --> 00:59:15,723
Rupture of the previously intact uterus during labor most often involves the thinned-out lower uterine segment.

494
00:59:15,723 --> 00:59:22,448
When the rent is in the immediate vicinity of the cervix, it frequently extends transversely or obliquely.

495
00:59:22,448 --> 00:59:29,248
When the rent forms in the portion of the uterus adjacent to the broad ligament, the tear is usually longitudinal .

496
00:59:29,248 --> 00:59:39,123
Although these tears develop primarily in the lower uterine segment, they can extend upward into the active segment or downward through the cervix and into the vagina.

497
00:59:39,123 --> 00:59:43,223
In some cases, the bladder also may be lacerated.

498
00:59:43,223 --> 00:59:46,085
Figure 42-13.

499
00:59:46,085 --> 00:59:49,035
Peripartum hysterectomy specimen.

500
00:59:49,035 --> 00:59:55,935
A right broad ligament hematoma is seen lateral to the closed hysterotomy incision and medial to the ovary.

501
00:59:55,935 --> 01:00:00,373
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Emily H. Adhikari.

502
01:00:00,373 --> 01:00:07,235
If the rupture is of sufficient size, the uterine contents will usually escape into the peritoneal cavity.

503
01:00:07,235 --> 01:00:15,198
If the presenting fetal part is firmly engaged, however, then only a portion of the fetus may be extruded from the uterus.

504
01:00:15,198 --> 01:00:30,998
Fetal prognosis is largely dependent on the size of the rent, the degree of placental separation, the magnitude of maternal hypovolemia, and the rapidity of diagnosis and response (Al-Zirqi, 2018; Rottenstreich, 2021).

505
01:00:30,998 --> 01:00:42,398
In some cases, the overlying peritoneum remains intact, and this usually is accompanied by hemorrhage that extends into the broad ligament to cause a large retroperitoneal hematoma.

506
01:00:42,398 --> 01:00:55,423
Following vaginal delivery in an unscarred uterus, we and others have occasionally encountered cases of an incomplete tear on the inside of the uterus that extends vertically into the active segment to cause profuse hemorrhage .

507
01:00:55,423 --> 01:01:03,948
These tears are usually not visible from below but are found at the time of hysterectomy for intractable bleeding despite a contracted uterus.

508
01:01:03,948 --> 01:01:12,710
Hemorrhage with this type of tear can be torrential, and bleeding is usually not slowed until the uterine artery pedicles are clamped bilaterally.

509
01:01:12,710 --> 01:01:15,623
# Management and Outcomes.

510
01:01:15,623 --> 01:01:24,523
The varied clinical presentations of uterine rupture and its management are discussed in detail in Chapter 31 (p. 579).

511
01:01:24,523 --> 01:01:36,698
In the most recent national maternal mortality statistics, uterine rupture accounted for nearly 10 percent of hemorrhage-associated deaths (Creanga, 2015, 2017).

512
01:01:36,698 --> 01:01:42,210
Maternal morbidity includes hysterectomy that may be necessary to control hemorrhage.

513
01:01:42,210 --> 01:01:53,673
Rates of perinatal mortality and morbidity, which may include severe neurological impairment, also are high (Al-Zirqi, 2018; Rottenstreich, 2021).

514
01:01:53,673 --> 01:02:00,148
Table 42-4 lists some perinatal outcomes in 72 women with uterine rupture.

515
01:02:00,148 --> 01:02:08,035
Last, maternal obesity comorbid with uterine rupture is associated with increased rates of adverse neonatal outcomes .

516
01:02:08,035 --> 01:02:17,898
With prior rupture and a subsequent pregnancy, Fox (2020) recommends cesarean delivery prior to labor or at the onset of preterm labor.

517
01:02:17,898 --> 01:02:20,523
Table 42-4.

518
01:02:20,523 --> 01:02:22,010
Some

519
01:02:22,180 --> 01:02:28,917
Maternal and Perinatal Outcomes in 72 Women with Uterine Rupture Amnionic Fluid Embolism.

520
01:02:28,867 --> 01:02:31,667
# Pathophysiology.

521
01:02:31,667 --> 01:02:38,155
This syndrome usually is caused by intravenous embolization of meconiumladen amnionic fluid.

522
01:02:38,155 --> 01:02:44,292
It results in rapid cardiorespiratory collapse and profound consumptive coagulopathy.

523
01:02:44,292 --> 01:02:51,105
The understanding of the mechanism of injury for amnionic fluid embolism (AFE) has evolved.

524
01:02:51,105 --> 01:03:02,542
Early theories proposed that amnionic fluid and debris entered the maternal circulation and obstructed pulmonary artery flow, which led to hypoxia, right heart failure, and death.

525
01:03:02,542 --> 01:03:13,292
However, during normal delivery, amnionic fluid commonly enters maternal circulation through venous channels at the placental implantation site or small lacerations.

526
01:03:13,292 --> 01:03:25,130
Accordingly, squames, fetal cells, and trophoblasts can often be identified in maternal peripheral blood at delivery (Clark, 1986; Lee, 1986).

527
01:03:25,130 --> 01:03:37,605
And, at least in experimental animals, infused clear amnionic fluid is generally innocuous, even in large amounts (Adamsons, 1971; Stolte, 1967).

528
01:03:37,605 --> 01:03:44,117
Instead, meconium-laden amnionic fluid is likely a potent cause of symptomatic AFE .

529
01:03:44,117 --> 01:03:51,367
Disruption of the maternal-fetal interface allows material from the fetal compartment to enter maternal circulation.

530
01:03:51,367 --> 01:04:05,592
This dyad leads to abnormal activation of proinflammatory mediator systems, similar to the systemic inflammatory response syndrome (SIRS), and causes initial, transient pulmonary vasoconstriction and hypertension .

531
01:04:05,592 --> 01:04:19,480
Pulmonary hypertension causes acute right ventricular failure, which is followed by hemodynamic collapse from right ventricular infarction, interventricular septum displacement, and decreased left-sided cardiac output .

532
01:04:19,480 --> 01:04:26,955
This right and then left ventricular dysfunction leads to cardiogenic pulmonary edema and systemic hypotension.

533
01:04:26,955 --> 01:04:32,092
Acute respiratory failure with severe hypoxemia from shunting develops.

534
01:04:32,092 --> 01:04:41,117
Notably, the resulting multiorgan dysfunction is an interrelated process, with both the cardiac and pulmonary systems affecting each other.

535
01:04:41,117 --> 01:04:49,830
Women who survive beyond these first phases invariably develop the third component of the classic triad—a consumptive coagulopathy.

536
01:04:49,830 --> 01:04:55,755
Material from the fetal compartment that contains tissue factor then activates factor VII.

537
01:04:55,755 --> 01:05:00,792
This leads to the development of disseminated intravascular coagulation.

538
01:05:00,792 --> 01:05:04,330
The rapidity of the coagulopathy is amazing .

539
01:05:04,330 --> 01:05:07,155
Figure 42-14.

540
01:05:07,155 --> 01:05:10,155
Fatal amnionic fluid embolism.

541
01:05:10,155 --> 01:05:20,467
Results of some coagulation studies show an abrupt drop in plasma fibrinogen levels and platelet count but an extremely elevated serum fibrin split product level.

542
01:05:20,467 --> 01:05:26,080
To those who succumb, postmortem histopathological findings may be obvious.

543
01:05:26,080 --> 01:05:33,805
However, detection of squames and keratin may require special stains, and even then, debris may not be seen.

544
01:05:33,805 --> 01:05:46,805
In one study, fetal elements were detected in 75 percent of autopsies and in 50 percent of specimens prepared from concentrated buffy coat aspirates taken antepartum from a pulmonary artery catheter .

545
01:05:46,805 --> 01:05:49,842
# Incidence and Risk Factors.

546
01:05:49,842 --> 01:06:02,142
Most reports describe an AFE frequency of 1 to 2 cases per 100,000 births (Clark, 2014; Fitzpatrick, 2019; Knight, 2010).

547
01:06:02,142 --> 01:06:07,692
The case-fatality rate in all of these studies ranges from 11 to 43 percent.

548
01:06:07,692 --> 01:06:22,517
From another perspective, AFE caused 5 to 15 percent of all pregnancy-related deaths in the United States, Canada, and France (Bonnet, 2018; Kramer, 2012; Petersen, 2019).

549
01:06:22,517 --> 01:06:34,867
Predisposing conditions are rapid labor, meconium-stained amnionic fluid, and tears into uterine and other large pelvic veins that permit an exchange of fluids between the maternal and fetal compartment .

550
01:06:34,867 --> 01:06:55,430
Other commonly cited risks include older maternal age; postterm pregnancy; labor induction or augmentation; eclampsia; cesarean, forceps, or vacuum delivery; placental abruption or previa; and hydramnios (Indraccolo, 2018; Knight, 2012; Kramer, 2012).

551
01:06:55,430 --> 01:07:07,030
The associations of uterine hypertonus appears to be the effect rather than the cause because uterine blood flow ceases when intrauterine pressures exceed 35 to 40 mm Hg.

552
01:07:07,030 --> 01:07:15,430
Thus, a hypertonic contraction would be the least likely circumstance for amnionic fluid and other debris to enter uterine veins .

553
01:07:15,430 --> 01:07:17,267
For this reason,

554
01:07:17,428 --> 01:07:21,328
hypertonus from oxytocin does not seem implicated.

555
01:07:21,278 --> 01:07:23,678
# Diagnosis.

556
01:07:23,678 --> 01:07:32,153
The classic triad of abrupt hemodynamic and respiratory compromise along with consumptive coagulopathy underpins its diagnosis .

557
01:07:32,153 --> 01:07:38,053
Proposed criteria for AFE diagnosis are shown in Table 42-5.

558
01:07:38,053 --> 01:07:45,153
In a French study, only 60 percent of women with autopsy-confirmed AFE had all four criteria .

559
01:07:45,153 --> 01:07:53,128
The classic example is dramatic, and a woman in the late stages of labor or immediately postpartum begins gasping for air.

560
01:07:53,128 --> 01:08:05,540
Seizures or cardiorespiratory arrest rapidly follow and are accompanied by massive hemorrhage from consumptive coagulopathy (Bernstein, 2019; Tanaka, 2017).

561
01:08:05,540 --> 01:08:20,353
Clinical manifestations vary, and we and others have managed several women in whom otherwise uncomplicated vaginal or cesarean delivery was followed by severe acute consumptive coagulopathy without overt cardiorespiratory difficulties.

562
01:08:20,353 --> 01:08:29,978
In these women, consumptive coagulopathy appears to be the forme fruste of AFE (Kramer, 2012; Porter, 1996).

563
01:08:29,978 --> 01:08:32,653
Table 42-5.

564
01:08:32,653 --> 01:08:42,103
Diagnostic Criteria for Amnionic Fluid Embolism In such cases, other sources of acute cardiac or respiratory failure should be considered.

565
01:08:42,103 --> 01:08:51,265
These include myocardial infarction, pulmonary or air embolism, high spinal blockage, eclampsia, and anaphylactic shock .

566
01:08:51,265 --> 01:08:56,340
In some cases, the temporal relationship of events aids diagnosis.

567
01:08:56,340 --> 01:09:06,190
Unfortunately, no specific diagnostic laboratory test confirms or refutes the diagnosis of AFE, and it remains a clinical diagnosis.

568
01:09:06,190 --> 01:09:17,940
Importantly, women suffering from excessive blood loss and resulting coagulopathy may be misdiagnosed with AFE, when the true culprit is unrecognized or underappreciated hemorrhage .

569
01:09:17,940 --> 01:09:24,190
In either event, a woman with cardiopulmonary compromise should receive immediate resuscitation .

570
01:09:24,190 --> 01:09:26,415
# Management.

571
01:09:26,415 --> 01:09:32,228
The initial period of systemic and pulmonary hypertension with AFE is transient.

572
01:09:32,228 --> 01:09:46,278
Thus, immediate high-quality cardiopulmonary resuscitation and advanced cardiac life support must be initiated without delay (Pacheco, 2020; Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine, 2016).

573
01:09:46,278 --> 01:09:51,228
These are discussed in detail in Chapter 50 (p. 897).

574
01:09:51,228 --> 01:09:57,578
Coagulopathy is managed as described in Chapter 44 (p. 777).

575
01:09:57,578 --> 01:10:03,590
If resuscitation is successful, hemodynamic instability is common in survivors.

576
01:10:03,590 --> 01:10:09,090
Both fever and hyperoxia will worsen ischemia-reperfusion injury to the brain.

577
01:10:09,090 --> 01:10:17,215
A suitable goal for temperature is 36°C and for mean arterial pressure is 65 mm Hg .

578
01:10:17,215 --> 01:10:22,203
Additional supportive care measures such as intubation are usually necessary.

579
01:10:22,203 --> 01:10:34,615
During the phase of right ventricular failure, inotropic agents such as dobutamine may improve right heart output, and later systemic hypotension should be treated with vasopressors such as norepinephrine.

580
01:10:34,615 --> 01:10:46,915
Excess fluid administration is discouraged because it can worsen dilation of an already engorged right ventricle, which may cause right-sided myocardial infarction and interventricular septum displacement.

581
01:10:46,915 --> 01:10:57,653
Extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO) has been described in the treatment of AFE (Bernstein, 2019; Webster, 2020).

582
01:10:57,653 --> 01:11:08,940
Further discussed in Chapter 50 (p. 886), venovenous ECMO is used for severe hypoxemic respiratory failure, without right or left ventricular failure.

583
01:11:08,940 --> 01:11:16,503
Venoarterial ECMO is less readily available and used for impending cardiac arrest and ventricular failure.

584
01:11:16,503 --> 01:11:25,578
One recent review of outcomes in 20 women with AFE managed with ECMO reported a maternal mortality rate of only 15 percent.

585
01:11:25,578 --> 01:11:31,678
The authors concluded that publication biases may have overestimated the favorable outcomes .

586
01:11:31,678 --> 01:11:42,590
Either immediately after cardiopulmonary collapse or during the ensuing phases of injury, a coagulopathy develops in most cases from activation of factor VII and X.

587
01:11:42,590 --> 01:11:48,440
This may be exacerbated by ongoing hemorrhage, and a common source is uterine atony.

588
01:11:48,440 --> 01:11:55,565
Thus, immediate evaluation of coagulation parameters is indicated with concurrent management of bleeding.

589
01:11:55,565 --> 01:11:58,228
# Clinical Outcomes.

590
01:11:58,228 --> 01:12:04,128
Most reports describe significant maternal and perinatal mortality with AFE.

591
01:12:04,128 --> 01:12:12,465
This is likely influenced by underdiagnosis and reporting biases that favor the most severe cases with the highest mortality rates.

592
01:12:12,465 --> 01:12:16,915
From a California database of 1.1 million deliveries,

593
01:12:17,068 --> 01:12:20,805
the mortality rate with AFE was 60 percent .

594
01:12:20,755 --> 01:12:26,780
In the INOSS database, maternal mortality rates ranged from 30 to 41 percent .

595
01:12:26,780 --> 01:12:33,143
Death can be rapid, and in one study, 12 of the 34 women died within 30 minutes .

596
01:12:33,143 --> 01:12:39,030
The mortality rate is highest in women who present with cardiac arrest—89 percent .

597
01:12:39,030 --> 01:12:43,293
Survivors commonly have profound neurological impairment.

598
01:12:43,293 --> 01:12:52,330
Clark (1995) observed that only 8 percent of women who lived despite cardiac arrest survived without neurological injury.

599
01:12:52,330 --> 01:13:01,230
In a report of 20 cases, the maternal mortality rate was 15 percent, and 40 percent of survivors had neurological disability .

600
01:13:01,230 --> 01:13:15,005
Overall, the prognosis appears to be more associated with disease severity and the attendant cardiac arrest than with any specific treatment modality (Clark, 2014; Fitzpatrick, 2019).

601
01:13:15,005 --> 01:13:23,718
As perhaps expected, perinatal outcomes also are poor and are inversely related to the maternal cardiac arrest-to-delivery interval.

602
01:13:23,718 --> 01:13:33,230
Even so, the neonatal survival rate is 70 percent, but unfortunately, up to half of survivors suffer residual neurological impairment.

603
01:13:33,230 --> 01:13:39,493
In a Canadian study, 28 percent of neonates were considered to be asphyxiated at birth .

